s say I discard B3. Then my hand has B6 (2), B8 (1). If I can get another B6, that's a Pung. The unshown B6 has 4, so possible. Alternatively, if I discard B8, then B6 (2) and B3 (1) are left. Same idea.\n\nBut which is better? Maybe discard B3 first. Or maybe discard B8. It's a toss-up. Alternatively, discard one of the F or J tiles.\n\nAnother angle: the hand has two T7s. If I discard one T7, I can keep one for a pair. The T7-8-9 Chow would still be valid. So Play T7 is an option. Let's see: if I discard T7, the hand becomes [W5, W6, W7, T7 (1), T8, T9, B3, B6 (2), B8, F1, F4, J1]. The T7-8-9 is still a valid Chow. The pair would be T7 (if I keep one). But I had two T7s, so after discarding one, I have one left. That could be part of a pair. So maybe this is a good move. By discarding T7, I reduce the number of non-Chow tiles (B3, B8, etc.), and keep the Chows intact.\n\nSo Play T7 might be a good choice. It allows me to maintain the two Chows and possibly form a pair later. The T7 is part of the T7-8-9 Chow, and after discarding one, I still have one left. So the Chow is still valid.\n\nTherefore, the best action might be to Play T7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential to form two Chows (W5-7 and T7-9). Discarding T7 maintains these Chows while reducing non-meld tiles. Keeping B3/B8 may hinder future melds. Discarding F1/J1 risks giving others scoring opportunities.  \nReason: Playing T7 preserves key Chows and creates a pair potential. It minimizes disruption to structured sequences and avoids discarding tiles that could aid opponents' melds.  \nAnswer: Play T7"}
